These assertions depend on happy-dom’s default `window.innerWidth` resolving to the `lg` breakpoint (1024px). That’s environment-dependent and can become flaky if the test runtime or default viewport changes. Set `window.innerWidth` explicitly in the test (and restore it) so breakpoint expectations are deterministic.
```suggestion
    const originalWidth = window.innerWidth;
    Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', { value: 1024, writable: true });

    const { result } = renderHook(() => useBreakpoint());

    expect(result.current.isAbove('xs')).toBe(true);
    expect(result.current.isAbove('sm')).toBe(true);

    Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', { value: originalWidth, writable: true });
  });

  it('isBelow returns correct values', () => {
    const originalWidth = window.innerWidth;
    Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', { value: 1024, writable: true });

    const { result } = renderHook(() => useBreakpoint());

    expect(result.current.isBelow('xs')).toBe(false);

    Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', { value: originalWidth, writable: true });
```

This test assumes the default viewport width maps to a breakpoint that is not in `hiddenOn: ['xs']` (the comment mentions happy-dom defaults to 1024/'lg'). To avoid flakiness across environments, explicitly set `window.innerWidth` to a known value for the breakpoint you want to exercise before rendering the hook.
```suggestion
    // Only hide on 'xs' — ensure the window width maps to a larger breakpoint
    const originalInnerWidth = window.innerWidth;

    try {
      Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', {
        value: 1024, // corresponds to 'lg' in our breakpoint system
        configurable: true,
      });

      const { result } = renderHook(() =>
        useResponsiveConfig({
          hiddenOn: ['xs'],
        }),
      );
      // With width 1024 ('lg'), the current breakpoint is not in hiddenOn, so not hidden
      expect(result.current.hidden).toBe(false);
    } finally {
      Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', {
        value: originalInnerWidth,
        configurable: true,
      });
    }
```

This assertion is intentionally non-deterministic (`undefined` OR `100`) because it depends on the test environment’s default breakpoint. That can hide real regressions. Prefer setting `window.innerWidth` to a known value and asserting a single expected result for that breakpoint.
```suggestion
  it('returns the value for a large (2xl) breakpoint when width is large', () => {
    const originalInnerWidth = window.innerWidth;

    Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', {
      writable: true,
      configurable: true,
      value: 4000,
    });

    window.dispatchEvent(new Event('resize'));

    const { result } = renderHook(() => useResponsive({ '2xl': 100 }));
    expect(result.current).toBe(100);

    Object.defineProperty(window, 'innerWidth', {
      writable: true,
      configurable: true,
      value: originalInnerWidth,
    });
```
